# ABI i386 entry: the state a process starts in
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Source: the i386 psABI, chapter "Low Level System Information", section
# "Process Initialization" (tables x87-fpucw, mxcsr-status, eflags-status and
# initial-stack), with the older published revision (abi386-4, chapter 3,
# "Process Stack and Registers") compared. What a test can see is the state in
# main(), after the C library's start-up code; where the rule is about the
# first instruction of the program that is an inference and the note says so.

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.001
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State
class      required
says       The stack pointer at process entry is word aligned: argc, the
           argument pointers and the environment pointers sit at addresses
           that are multiples of 4.
note       Inferred. The initial ESP is derived from the address of argv[0]
           (argc is at ESP, argv starts 4 bytes above). Holds in both
           published revisions (the older one: word aligned).
test       t/001-initial-stack-word-aligned.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.002
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State
class      disputed
says       The stack pointer at process entry is guaranteed to be 16-byte
           aligned.
note       Follows the current revision (16 bytes). The older published
           revision (abi386-4) guarantees word (4-byte) alignment only, so a
           FAIL is INSPECT; entry.001 holds in both. Inferred from argv as in
           entry.001.
test       t/002-initial-stack-16.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.003
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State
class      required
says       The argument count is not negative, and the array of argument
           strings has a null pointer after the last one.
note       Both revisions.
test       t/003-argv-terminated.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.004
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State (table initial-stack)
class      required
says       On the initial stack the environment pointers come right after the
           null pointer that ends the argument pointers, and end with a null
           pointer.
note       Inferred from the table of the initial stack: it presumes the
           envp that main() receives points into that block.
test       t/004-envp-layout.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.005
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Auxiliary Vector (table aux-vec)
class      required
says       After the environment pointers the auxiliary vector follows, as
           pairs of 32-bit words ending with an entry of type AT_NULL; the
           entries a process can check against its own system calls (page
           size, clock ticks, the four user and group IDs, secure-mode flag,
           program-header entry size) carry the right values.
note       Only entries that are present are checked; the table lists more
           types than every system supplies, and unknown types are ignored.
test       t/005-auxv.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.006
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Special Registers (table eflags-status)
class      required
says       The direction flag is clear at process start and at every function
           entry, so it is clear in main().
note       Both revisions. Observed first thing in main().
test       t/006-df-clear-in-main.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.007
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Special Registers (table x87-fpucw)
class      required
says       At start the x87 control word selects round-to-nearest and masks
           all six exceptions (precision, underflow, overflow, zero divide,
           denormal operand, invalid operation).
note       Both revisions agree on these fields. Observed in main().
test       t/007-x87-cw-masks.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.008
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Special Registers (table x87-fpucw)
class      disputed
says       At start the x87 precision control field is 11, the extended
           (64-bit mantissa) precision.
note       Follows the current revision. The older revision gives the same
           bits (11) but labels them 53-bit double precision, so its table
           contradicts itself; a FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/008-x87-cw-precision.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.009
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Special Registers (table mxcsr-status)
class      required
says       At start the MXCSR control bits are: all six exceptions masked,
           rounding to nearest, flush-to-zero off, denormals-are-zero off.
note       Current revision only (the older one predates SSE). Observed in
           main(). Needs a processor with SSE.
test       t/009-mxcsr-control.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.010
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Special Registers
class      disputed
says       At start the exception status flags of both floating-point units
           (x87 status word and MXCSR) are clear.
note       Disputed because the state is read in main(), after constructors and other
           start-up code may have run, not at the entry point; such code may do
           inexact arithmetic and still leave a conforming process entry, so a FAIL
           is INSPECT.
test       t/010-fp-status-clear.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.011
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Registers
class      disputed
says       The x87 register stack is empty at a function entry, so when
           main() starts.
note       Stated outright by the older revision (the x87 registers are empty
           before entry to a function). The current revision requires only
           that the processor is in x87 mode at function entry, which an
           empty stack satisfies; so a FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/011-x87-empty-in-main.c
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.012
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State (EDX)
class      required
says       EDX at the first instruction holds a function pointer that the
           application is expected to register to run at exit. When it is
           not zero it is the address of code that can be registered and run
           at a normal exit.
note       Observed at the true entry: the test is linked with an entry point
           of its own that stores EDX and jumps to the ordinary start symbol
           (xflags). Only what the text states is checked. Zero is not
           judged (the text gives it no meaning), so a zero EDX reports
           NOTINUSE. A non-zero value is registered in a child process which
           then exits normally; a value that is not callable code ends the
           child by a signal. If the linker refuses the entry option the
           test is built without it and reports NOTINUSE.
test       t/012-edx-at-entry.c
xflags     -Wl,-e,abi_entry_stub
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.013
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State (EBP)
class      untestable
reason     The text says the content of EBP at process entry is unspecified and asks the user code to set the frame pointer to zero to mark the deepest frame. That places a duty on the program's own start-up code, not a promise by the system, so there is no system behaviour to assert; whatever EBP holds can be seen but no value is right or wrong. A walk of the saved frame pointers is not a check either, since compiled code need not keep a frame pointer.
says       The deepest stack frame is marked by a frame pointer of zero,
           which user code sets.

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.014
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Stack State (table initial-stack)
class      disputed
says       At the first instruction ESP is 16-byte aligned, the word at ESP is
           the argument count and the argument pointers follow it.
note       Observed at the true entry through an entry point of the test's own
           (see entry.012), so it needs no inference from argv. Follows the
           current revision (16 bytes); the older revision guarantees word
           alignment only, so a FAIL is INSPECT (entry.001 and entry.002 are
           the inferred counterparts). That main() receives the same array is
           assumed.
test       t/014-esp-at-entry.c
xflags     -Wl,-e,abi_entry_stub
nomutant   observes the state a process starts in; no library function to plant a fault in

assertion  ABI.i386.entry.015
anchor     i386-psabi-older:abi386-4#Object Files/ELF Header (Machine Information)
class      disputed
says       The program's own file identifies as a 32-bit (class 1),
           little-endian file (data encoding 1) for the i386 architecture
           (machine 3) with no processor flags (zero).
note       Only the older published revision states this; the current source
           has no ELF header section. A FAIL is INSPECT. The test reads the
           file named by its absolute argv[0] with a small reader; if argv[0]
           is not an absolute path the setup is UNRESOLVED. Other ELF facts
           (the interpreter name, the entry address) are not psABI-checkable;
           see STATUS.md.
test       t/015-elf-header.c
nomutant   reads a file the system built; no library function to plant a fault in
